  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n- We start with the provided initial_h_values (n=80) as a base solution\n- Use hierarchical optimization: first coarsen to a simpler representation (n=40) to identify core structure, then densify progressively (n=80,160,320) to improve solution\n- Each level builds on the previous by:\n  - Projection to preserve integral constraint\n  - Iterative refinement with normalization\n  - Selection of best candidate\n- Core idea: find a step function that minimizes max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx by exploiting state-of-the-art optimization techniques including:\n  - Multilevel optimization to transfer solutions between resolutions\n  - Local search with gradient-based methods at each level\n  - Repair mechanisms to maintain constraints\n- Address bottleneck: optimizing complex high-dimensional non-convex problem with expensive correlation evaluations\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n- Representation:\n  Let h \u2208 [0,1]^{n_points} represent step function samples at equally spaced points dx=2.0/spacing\n  Constraint: sum(h)*dx = 1 (integral constraint)\n- Optimization objective:\n  C5 = max_{shifts} [ (cross-correlation between h and (1-h)) * dx ]\n  Mode='full' without circular assumption\n- Projection operator (change-resolution function):\n  Proj_{n_points}(h) = upsample using cubic interpolation preserving mass\n- Constraint handling:\n  Resampling must ensure continuous integral preservation \u2192 post-normalization\n- Optimization method at resolution n:\n  Stochastic gradient descent with:\n    - Directional perturbations for autonomy\n    - Temperature-exponential annealing for diversity\n    - Line search gradient steps\n    - Repair by local projection\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n- Parameters: spcaies = [320,160,80] (in that order)\n- Procedure:\n  a) Start with initial_h_values (80 samples)\n  b) Down-project to 40 samples via:\n          h40 = project_halving(initial_h_values)\n  c) For target_resolution in [320,1